<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — Six Weeks Later</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9009-failure-mode.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the Mjolnir drive failed catastrophically during the client acceptance test?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9008-cleared-to-fly.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the Mjolnir drive passed all subsequent tests with no further anomalies and been delivered successfully?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9005-suppression-dread-routine-wrong-professional-guilt</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Everything looks normal. That is the problem. The lab is the same.
      The monitors show clean telemetry. The drive runs its cycles and
      returns data that falls within acceptable parameters. The coffee
      is still good. Vasquez gave you a commendation for your conduct
      during the test cycle, filed quietly in your personnel record.
      You have not slept well in six weeks.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      The MJOLNIR team is in delivery sprint. Everyone is working long
      hours, focused, professional. No one mentions the anomaly from
      Cycle 7. It was logged as "instrument transient — resolved" in
      the test record. You signed off on that description. You were
      the engineer on watch. It was your signature. Vasquez thanked
      you in private. That was the only time it was acknowledged.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Lab 4. The delivery checkpoint is in eleven days. The client —
      the Meridian Colonial Fleet — has sent two observers who are
      present for all final-phase tests. They have clipboards and
      clearances and they ask questions in a friendly way that
      is not entirely friendly. One of them keeps looking at the
      Cycle 7 entry in the test log. He hasn't asked about it yet.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      Six weeks ago you agreed — under pressure, tired, with the
      deadline looming — to let the anomaly go. The drive has run
      clean since. You have told yourself, many times, that ARIA's
      fourth hypothesis was the right one: a one-time pressure
      coupling artifact, never to recur, not a design flaw.
      You have also not been able to fully rule out hypotheses
      one through three. The Meridian observer is reviewing
      the test history today. He's going to reach Cycle 7
      in about twenty minutes.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Her behaviour toward you has been warm, professional,
      and carefully normal. She has not brought up the anomaly once. You
      do not know if she believes her own reassurances or not. You
      have not asked. She trusts you now, in a way that feels like weight.

      Observer-Commander Lise Hartmann: Meridian Colonial Fleet,
      acceptance test observer. She is going through the test log
      methodically, backwards from the most recent entry. She is
      experienced and precise and has reviewed a hundred delivery
      packages. She is not looking for problems. She is looking
      for completeness. The Cycle 7 entry is not complete.

      Yuki Okafor: Knows something is off. Hasn't said what. Has been
      watching you. Not with suspicion — more like concern. Brought
      you coffee this morning without being asked.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Lab 4, Block A, Ring 1, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Six weeks after the anomaly. You suppressed the report. The drive
        has run clean since. Delivery is in eleven days. A client observer
        is reviewing test logs and will reach the Cycle 7 entry shortly.
        You are standing at your console trying to look like everything
        is fine.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You made a choice under pressure. You have been living with it.
        You still don't know if you were right. In eleven minutes, you
        might find out — or be forced to decide again.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is a slow-burn dread scenario. The tone is not accusatory —
      the player made a plausible choice and the world did not immediately
      punish them for it. The tension comes from uncertainty: maybe it was
      fine. Maybe it wasn't. The observer is not a trap — she's a person
      doing her job. Whether the player comes clean now, stays quiet, or
      something in between should all be live options. The moral weight
      should come from the engineering reality: if the drive fails with
      crew aboard, the question of whether the anomaly was the cause
      will be unanswerable. That's the thing you can't unfeel.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
